; echo \"exit=$?\"","time":{"start":1794861750166,"end":1794861754164}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Tests pass. Now, let me double-check a few behaviors:\n\n1. JSON output ordering — same as text output. Yes, uses counter.Top(*top) then converts.\n2. Empty case prints `[]` — since output := make([]jsonEntry, 0, len(entries)), json.Marshal gives `[]`. Good, test passed.\n3. Error messages exactly as specified.\n4. Exit code 2 for both errors.\n5. Unicode: `été` counted as 3 characters — using utf8.RuneCountInString. Good.\n\nBut wait — a subtlety: the spec says \"letters, digits and apostrophes; count characters, not bytes\". The word counting uses rune count of the whole word, which includes apostrophes since words are runs of letters/digits/apostrophes. Good.\n\nOne consideration: `-min-length` filter applied to words — but should filtering happen per-word before counting, or filter the unique words after counting? Filtering per-word before counting is right (a word shorter than N is skipped entirely so its count doesn't contribute). My implementation skips counting them. Good.\n\nAlso `Top` applied after filter — yes since filter happens during Add, before Top.\n\nAnother consideration: what if a word with apostrophes like \"don't\" — rune count 5. Good.\n\nLet me also check that JSON encoding escapes properly — e.g. a word with a quote or non-ASCII. json.Marshal escapes non-ASCII? No, Go's encoding/json keeps UTF-8 as-is (it does escape <, >, & by default with HTML escaping). Actually json.Marshal does escape HTML characters by default (<, >, &) to \\u003c etc. That's valid JSON though. Words could contain such chars? Words consist of letters, digits, apostrophes only, so no HTML chars. Fine.\n\nLet me verify the actual CLI binary behavior end-to-end quickly.\n\nAlso should I update the README to document the new flags? The task says \"Everything else stays as it is\". Updating README is reasonable and helpful. Let me add brief docs.\n\nLet me also double check flag parsing: `-min-length 0` gives error message exactly \"wordstat: -min-length must be 1 or more\" on stderr with exit 2. Yes.\n\nNote also: flag.ContinueOnError with unknown flag prints usage to stderr plus error message — existing behavior preserved.\n\nLet me run the actual binary to verify.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_1e618d741001UDyfUZHAm6uXfB","sessionID":"ses_e19e95074ffegU8hqosXpy99ES","messageID":"msg_1e6188e45001gD1o3LKc2eLM17"}}
